WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.132
TypeScript type checking happens before the application runs.

2
00:00:04.132 --> 00:00:07.729
That is valuable, but it is not the same as runtime trust.

3
00:00:07.729 --> 00:00:13.468
A type declaration can say that a request has an email field, a role field, or a count field.

4
00:00:13.468 --> 00:00:26.018
It cannot prove that a JSON body, URL parameter, database record, local storage value, third-party response, message event, or environment variable actually has the expected value at runtime.

5
00:00:26.018 --> 00:00:30.303
Defensive TypeScript code treats external data as unknown first.

6
00:00:30.303 --> 00:00:35.124
unknown is useful because it forces code to narrow and validate before use.

7
00:00:35.124 --> 00:00:40.557
Boundary parsing should convert untrusted data into validated application types.

8
00:00:40.557 --> 00:00:48.056
After parsing, the rest of the code can work with a stronger type that represents a value the application has checked.

9
00:00:48.056 --> 00:00:50.046
Unsafe type escapes need care.

10
00:00:50.046 --> 00:00:59.917
any, unchecked type assertions, overly broad generics, and non-null assertions can silence the compiler at exactly the moment the team needs feedback.

11
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:08.411
Sometimes an assertion is reasonable near a well-tested framework boundary, but it should be local, justified, and easy to review.

12
00:01:08.411 --> 00:01:18.665
Schema validation, parsing functions, discriminated unions, narrow types, and explicit conversion functions all help make trust transitions deliberate.

13
00:01:18.665 --> 00:01:20.961
The point is not to create busywork.

14
00:01:20.961 --> 00:01:27.006
The point is to separate what the outside world claims from what the application has verified.

15
00:01:27.006 --> 00:01:29.761
A TypeScript type is a promise made by code.

16
00:01:29.761 --> 00:01:33.587
Runtime validation is how outside data earns that promise.

17
00:01:33.587 --> 00:01:37.413
This pattern should be consistent across the application.

18
00:01:37.413 --> 00:01:46.290
A route handler, worker event, configuration loader, and API client should all have a clear place where raw values become trusted values.

19
00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:56.467
When the boundary is hidden or skipped, later code may look safe because it has strong TypeScript types, while the actual runtime value has never been checked.

20
00:01:56.467 --> 00:02:03.125
Consistent boundary parsing makes reviews faster and makes tests easier to place for every input path.
